## Types as design enforcement

Most teams use TypeScript to catch `undefined`. Design engineers use it to make **off-system design impossible to write**.

```ts
type Space = 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 6 | 8 | 12 | 16;
type Tone = "accent" | "neutral" | "danger" | "success";

interface ButtonProps {
  size: "sm" | "md" | "lg";
  tone?: Tone;          // no raw hex ever reaches a component
  iconOnly?: boolean;
}
```

A developer physically cannot pass `padding={13}` or `color="#ff0000"`. The design system stops being a PDF people ignore and becomes a compiler error people fix.

## Variants without chaos

Discriminated unions model real component states better than boolean soup:

```ts
type CardProps =
  | { variant: "project"; project: Project }
  | { variant: "article"; article: Article; readTime: number };
```

No more `isArticle && hasProject` impossible states. Each variant declares exactly what it needs — and autocomplete teaches the system to every new teammate for free.

## satisfies: config with guarantees

```ts
const THEME = {
  accent: "#B91942",
  bgBase: "#FAF7F6",
} satisfies Record<string, \`#${string}\`>;
```

`satisfies` validates the shape while preserving the literal values — so `THEME.accent` stays typed as its exact string, and a typo'd key fails the build instead of shipping.

## The payoff

Every constraint you encode is a review comment you never write again. On a two-person team that's convenience; across a growing product it's the difference between a design system and a design suggestion.

## From the field: the prop that ended design review comments

A startup's design reviews were 40% "wrong spacing / wrong color" comments. We shipped a typed component kit where every visual prop was a union of token names — then watched review threads shrink. Two sprints later the team measured it: visual-consistency comments dropped by roughly three quarters, and the remaining ones were *real* design debates, not policing. The compiler had absorbed the policing.

The unexpected win was onboarding: a contractor shipped brand-consistent screens on day two, because autocomplete offered only valid choices. The type system had become the design system's distribution channel.

## Three patterns worth stealing verbatim

**Token-typed CSS custom properties:**

```ts
type TokenVar = `var(--${string})`;
const glow = (t: TokenVar) => `0 0 24px ${t}`; // raw hex rejected at compile time
```

**Exhaustive variant rendering** — add a variant, get compile errors at every unhandled site:

```ts
function icon(v: "info" | "warn" | "error") {
  switch (v) {
    case "info": return InfoIcon;
    case "warn": return WarnIcon;
    case "error": return ErrorIcon;
    default: { const never: never = v; return never; }
  }
}
```

**Responsive prop objects** typed against your breakpoint scale — `{ base: 2, md: 4 }` compiles, `{ tablet: 3 }` doesn't.

None of these require advanced TypeScript. They require deciding that the design system deserves the same enforcement as the API layer.

